## 1. EXECUTIVE VERDICT

Following an exhaustive forensic audit across all subsystems of **Tracker 360**, the application's overall technical health is diagnosed with **systemic architectural fragmentation and dual-authority state divergence**, rather than isolated visual UI bugs.

The core subsystems—**Task Management (`todos`), Backlog Planning (`allBacklogs`), Syllabus Mastery (`syllabus`), XP Accounting (`xp`), Daily Question Tracking (`questionsSolved`), and Cloud Persistence (`Cloud Firestore`)**—operate under disjoint data models with uncoordinated mutation lifecycles:

1. **Dual-Model Authority Contradiction:** Tracker 360 maintains tasks in two independent, unlinked schemas:
   - The flat dashboard checklist (`todos: Todo[]` in [`AppContext.tsx`](file:///c:/Users/pc/Downloads/levelup-study%20%285%29/src/context/AppContext.tsx)).
   - The hierarchical chapter backlog (`syllabus: Subject[]` and `allBacklogs` in [`BacklogHQ.tsx`](file:///c:/Users/pc/Downloads/levelup-study%20%285%29/src/pages/BacklogHQ.tsx)).
   Mutations in one subsystem do not propagate to the other. Checking off a chapter lecture in [`Syllabus.tsx`](file:///c:/Users/pc/Downloads/levelup-study%20%285%29/src/pages/Syllabus.tsx) never awards XP or marks scheduled tasks complete; checking a task on the dashboard never advances syllabus chapter mastery.
2. **Monolithic Document Anti-Pattern on Firebase Spark:** State synchronization serializes the entire user state (todos, syllabus, history, settings, sessions) into a single document at `/users/{userId}`. A real-time `onSnapshot` listener on this monolithic document creates an infinite read/write feedback loop during multi-device or multi-tab sessions, exhausting the Spark free tier (20,000 writes/day) in less than three hours.
3. **Cross-Project Access Failure (Mitigated):** The codebase was hardcoded in [`firebase-applet-config.json`](file:///c:/Users/pc/Downloads/levelup-study%20%285%29/firebase-applet-config.json) to an unauthorized/expired GCP project (`waking-journey-pv7sv`), causing HTTP 403 authorization rejections on all Firestore requests. Cloud synchronization was completely blocked, trapping task mutations in device-local `localStorage`. This has been resolved and pointed to the authenticated project `o-auth-1` (`807015900608`).
4. **Touch Gesture Hijacking on Mobile Calendar:** Bounding-box pointer events in [`StudyCalendar.tsx`](file:///c:/Users/pc/Downloads/levelup-study%20%285%29/src/components/StudyCalendar.tsx) lack touch-action discrimination and velocity dampening. Natural vertical swipe gestures exceed a tiny 6-pixel threshold, hijacking the native touch scroll and spuriously triggering task creation modals.
5. **Strict Code Preservation:** Per the directive (`note:- don't change any code`), no production `.ts`/`.tsx` files have been modified. All findings, causal proofs, and non-destructive architectural specifications have been committed to four authoritative deliverables in `docs/`:
